-    $$ c = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{G}} c_i \alpha_i \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \alpha_j) $$
-    where $c$ is the rendered color, $\mathcal{G}$ is the set of 3D Gaussians, and the product term represents transmittance [S2].
+    a. Initialize $\Delta \mu^{prev}_i = 0$.
+    b. Recursively execute the update rules (Step 6a-6d) twice to obtain a converged $\Delta \mu_i$ [S2].
+    c. Use the converged point $\mu_i$ to query $F$ and decode $\alpha_i$ and $\Sigma_i$ [S2].
+
+8.  **Color Prediction:**
+    Project the 3D Gaussians to the $K$ nearest reference views to retrieve 2D context (color windows $\{c_k\}_{k=1}^K$ and visibility maps). Decode the Gaussian color $c_i$ using this 2D context [S2].
+
+9.  **Background Modeling:**
+    Model distant views and the sky using a generalizable hemisphere Gaussian model. Geometry is fixed, and color is predicted similarly to the foreground volume [S2].
+
+10. **Rendering:**
+    Compose foreground and background Gaussians. Render the image using standard alpha blending:
+    $$ c = \sum_{i \in G} c_i \alpha_i \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \alpha_j) $$
+    where $G$ is the set of sorted 3D Gaussians [S2].
 
 **Parameters:**
-*   $N$: Number of input images [S2].
-*   $N_p$: Number of primitives in the global point cloud [S2].
-*   $K$: Number of nearest reference views for color retrieval [S2].
-*   $v_{size}$: Voxel size used to scale the position offset output [S2].
